\chapter{Formatter, diagnostics, and repair proposals}
\label{ch:50}
\index{Formatter, diagnostics, and repair proposals}
\index{Diagnostics!scrutiny}
\index{Diagnostics!repair}

Use \OnxCodeTerm{on1xc fmt INPUT} for plain source output and \OnxCodeTerm{--check} for formatting checks. \OnxCodeTerm{--inplace} rewrites the source, and format targets include LaTeX or troff. The formatter uses \OnxCodeTerm{.on1xfmt} and avoids evaluating project scripts or PPX. It operates on source form, so successful formatting does not imply successful elaboration or backend lowering.

Compiler diagnostics have both a bounded catalog-backed path and legacy messages. The newer path supports policy such as \OnxCodeTerm{@scrutiny} and registered advisory delivery. In an eligible build, Wetstone supplies bounded static analysis and Syren emits supported JSON/SARIF output and repair proposals. A repair written to a separate path can be independently rechecked. Compare the edited source and run behavior tests even if the compiler accepts it. A range of unchecked or skipped HIR remains outside the advisory result. \cref{ch:89} follows the independent checkers.

\section{Explain a formatting discrepancy}
Suppose two source files in one project produce different brace layouts. Begin at each input file's own directory and walk up to the nearest \OnxCodeTerm{.on1xfmt}. The first file found wins as a whole. A nearer file specifying only the preferred width does not inherit a more distant choice of brace style. Running the formatter from the same shell directory for both files is therefore insufficient to establish that both used the same settings. Equally, a long string on a line wider than the preferred width is not by itself a formatting failure: changing the literal to meet the width could change the program.

Test the discrepancy in two steps. Format to standard output and compare that output with the source. Then use the formatter's check mode on plain source. For valid input, a difference gives status one, while an unchanged file gives zero. Neither comparison requires importing the library named by a function call. Consequently a misspelled module name can survive a successful formatting check and still fail during ordinary compilation.

For a deliberately aligned region, surround it with paired standalone formatter off and on comments. The intervening spelling is preserved, including the directive lines. This is a layout control, not conditional compilation: an unmatched delimiter cannot be hidden from the parser by putting it between the comments. When evaluating a proposed repair, ask both whether it is syntactically acceptable and whether it changes the intended code inside a carefully preserved region.

